1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,792
It is Sunday evening, and Aziz wants a washing machine on credit.

2
00:00:03,842 --> 00:00:09,384
He opens the mobile bank, taps the credit offer, and the app stops him with a screen full of text.

3
00:00:09,434 --> 00:00:14,280
Before any camera opens, the screen tells him what the bank is about to read about him.

4
00:00:14,330 --> 00:00:19,400
Why does a screen come first, and not the face check?

5
00:00:19,450 --> 00:00:26,480
In Lesson 13, Aziz's password became a digest that nobody can reverse, with a lock for wrong guesses.

6
00:00:26,530 --> 00:00:29,984
Everything so far proves his phone, not his person.

7
00:00:30,034 --> 00:00:37,376
This lesson adds the first step towards level two: the consent gate, the screen that stands before every call to MyID.

8
00:00:37,426 --> 00:00:39,344
Look at the screen with Aziz.

9
00:00:39,394 --> 00:00:41,216
It has four fixed parts.

10
00:00:41,266 --> 00:00:53,120
The first part is the data: his names, his date and place of birth, his personal identification number, gender, citizenship, nationality, document data, registered addresses and phone number.

11
00:00:53,170 --> 00:00:56,000
The second part is the provider: MyID.

12
00:00:56,050 --> 00:01:00,608
The third part is the purpose: to identify him as a customer of this bank.

13
00:01:00,658 --> 00:01:05,528
The fourth part says who can read the data later: the bank's channels and its operators.

14
00:01:05,578 --> 00:01:11,600
The bank writes the words of each part, in the language of the journey, but it cannot remove a part.

15
00:01:11,650 --> 00:01:15,512
And the screen shows only after Aziz's phone number is verified.

16
00:01:15,562 --> 00:01:19,448
Aziz reads the fourth line twice, and taps Refuse.

17
00:01:19,498 --> 00:01:23,320
What happens now?

18
00:01:23,370 --> 00:01:25,192
Nothing at MyID.

19
00:01:25,242 --> 00:01:30,520
The platform opens no session, counts no attempt, and keeps Aziz at level one.

20
00:01:30,570 --> 00:01:32,848
It writes one event: refused.

21
00:01:32,898 --> 00:01:38,992
I think this is the most honest choice in the whole proofing journey: saying no costs the customer nothing.

22
00:01:39,042 --> 00:01:43,816
Ten minutes later, Aziz changes his mind, and taps the credit offer again.

23
00:01:43,866 --> 00:01:45,688
The screen comes back.

24
00:01:45,738 --> 00:01:49,792
Nothing is pre-selected, and nothing is copied from his first answer.

25
00:01:49,842 --> 00:01:51,664
This time he taps Accept.

26
00:01:51,714 --> 00:01:55,840
Before any other step, the platform writes an acceptance record.

27
00:01:55,890 --> 00:02:05,008
It holds the identifiers and the versions of the consent text and the terms text, the language, the date and time, the device, and Aziz.

28
00:02:05,058 --> 00:02:08,080
Only then can the platform open a session at MyID.

29
00:02:08,130 --> 00:02:11,416
And one acceptance permits one session, no more.

30
00:02:11,466 --> 00:02:16,216
The record points to the session it allowed, so nobody can use a yes two times.

31
00:02:16,266 --> 00:02:19,576
The gate is not a one-time screen at registration.

32
00:02:19,626 --> 00:02:26,392
It stands before every call to MyID: a step-up, a new device, a dormant account, and a refresh.

33
00:02:26,442 --> 00:02:31,048
Next year, when Aziz moves to a new phone, he sees the gate again.

34
00:02:31,098 --> 00:02:32,920
Now the versions.

35
00:02:32,970 --> 00:02:38,392
Each consent text and each terms text exists in the four languages of the installation.

36
00:02:38,442 --> 00:02:44,824
Each version has a publication date, the staff user who proposed it, and a different staff user who approved it.

37
00:02:44,874 --> 00:02:51,568
The product ships a default text, the bank's compliance team can change it, and the platform keeps every version.

38
00:02:51,618 --> 00:02:54,136
Here is the case that this protects.

39
00:02:54,186 --> 00:02:57,928
Aziz opens the screen and starts to read, slowly.

40
00:02:57,978 --> 00:03:02,704
Two minutes later, compliance publishes a new version, with a changed purpose.

41
00:03:02,754 --> 00:03:06,832
Three minutes after that, Aziz taps Accept on the old text.

42
00:03:06,882 --> 00:03:10,384
The platform refuses the answer and shows him the new text.

43
00:03:10,434 --> 00:03:14,256
Why so strict?

44
00:03:14,306 --> 00:03:19,104
Because the acceptance record must name exactly the version that Aziz saw.

45
00:03:19,154 --> 00:03:24,864
If the bank must ever show what he agreed to, the record and the text must match, word for word.

46
00:03:24,914 --> 00:03:32,856
On the map, the consent step lives in the customer gateway, and the acceptance records live in the record store, next to the customer.

47
00:03:32,906 --> 00:03:36,336
The mobile bank only shows the screen and sends the answer.

48
00:03:36,386 --> 00:03:46,104
To recap: four fixed parts, one answer before every call to MyID, a refusal that costs nothing, and an acceptance record that names the exact version.

49
00:03:46,154 --> 00:03:47,976
Aziz has said yes.

50
00:03:48,026 --> 00:03:49,848
Now the camera can open.

51
00:03:49,898 --> 00:03:55,784
But who opens the session at MyID: the mobile bank, or the platform?

52
00:03:55,834 --> 00:03:59,096
And what happens when the marketplace asks for the same check?

53
00:03:59,146 --> 00:04:01,136
Next: proofing with MyID.
